Vacuum drops records superseded by writes that are not durable yet. A power
cut then loses the superseding write and the record it replaced, and the key
holds neither value. Reverts #261 (#245).
It takes vacuum dropping a record, sync=false writes that superseded it and
a power cut before they are synced, so every life ends in a power cut and
nothing slows the workload down:
Budget: 10
Disable: sigkill,clean,read_eio,meta_eio,short,enospc,erofs,latency,stall,rlimit_as,rlimit_nofile
The rig caught it in 2 of 5 runs of 10 minutes, focused or not; the guard
cuts the power right after the vacuum.
Guarded-by: "vacuum does not drop a durable record that only an unsynced write supersedes"

diff --git a/bytecaskdb/bytecask.cppm b/bytecaskdb/bytecask.cppm
index aab1a99e..a060b39e 100644
--- a/bytecaskdb/bytecask.cppm
+++ b/bytecaskdb/bytecask.cppm
@@ -3884,11 +3884,6 @@ void DB::vacuum_commit(std::uint32_t old_file_id,
   // unlinked, but what superseded it may be a sync=false write a crash can
   // still lose, taking the key's last durable value with it (#245). So
   // everything the scan saw becomes durable before the source can go.
-  if (t.durable_seq() < decided_at) {
-    // A sync after a failed one proves nothing (#231).
-    if (current->degraded) throw DbDegraded{current->degraded_reason};
-    sync_active_file(t, current, "vacuum");
-  }
   t.apply_vacuum(old_file_id, scan, std::move(new_sealed_file), dest_file_id);
 
   store_state(current, std::move(t).persistent());
